// src/v4/extdeps/languages/verilog.dag
//
// Scope: Verilog HDL model — direction-agnostic. Hardware description:
//        modules, ports, wire/reg, continuous assignment, always-blocks,
//        module instantiation.
// Anchor: IEEE 1364-2005 (IEEE Standard for Verilog HDL) — the versioned
//         fidelity authority the worker models against (NOT Wikipedia).
//         https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verilog is accessible context only.
// Stress (T-4.9 — a B2-OMNI falsification probe): hardware CONCURRENCY vs
//   the 5 L1 behaviors. `always @(posedge clk)` and continuous assignment
//   are concurrent dataflow, NOT sequential control flow. The IN-B
//   decision (concurrency = Bind composition + effect typing, NO 6th L1
//   behavior — coordination.dag) is VALIDATED here: if Verilog cannot be
//   modeled without a 6th `Concurrent` behavior, that is a C1 stop-signal
//   escalation. Catching that now — before the substrate is load-bearing
//   — is precisely the point of adding this early.
// Clear win: ONE `.dag` FSM → simulable Verilog + a Rust reference model,
//   same Node, zero translator (hardware + software from one source).
//
// Owns:
//   - Verilog grammar (module / port / wire / reg / assign / always /
//     instantiation) as declarative data (B2 — generic walker, not code)
//   - Concurrency expressed as effect-typed Bind composition (per IN-B;
//     NOT a new behavior — that would be a C1 STOP)
//   - Inhabitance: bit-vectors (wire/reg [N:0]) per std/algebra.dag
//   - Cost realization: per-construct cost on a Verilog target
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag, std/algebra.dag, std/primitive.dag
//   - extdeps/coordination.dag (concurrency via effect-typed Bind — IN-B)
//
// Status: T-4.9 modeled 2026-05-16 (bright-crane-527). IN-B probe RESULT:
//   PASS — Verilog hardware concurrency models cleanly under the existing
//   five L1 behaviors with NO 6th `Concurrent` behavior and NO core/
//   pipeline change (see modeling notes "IN-B PROBE FINDING"). The probe
//   surfaces the structurally-correct intra-vs-cross-program partition:
//   hardware concurrency (parallel `always`-blocks / continuous assigns
//   within ONE module) is the Conj-of-parallel-siblings + Bind-chain
//   shape, with per-language effect-typing intrinsic to the wire/reg/
//   vector signal type — exactly the THESIS:401 IN-B disposition.
//   The scaffold-header `Consumes` line is preserved verbatim and carries
//   two known stale citations: (1) `std/primitive.dag` was deleted by
//   PR #3152 (STRUCTURE.md §"Scalar/numeric concept decomposition"); the
//   six concept-anchored scalar files are the replacement, and `Symbol`
//   is now substrate-ambient at std/node.dag (K-1). (2) `extdeps/
//   coordination.dag` is an unfilled scaffold (T-4.8) and its modeled
//   scope is CROSS-PROGRAM coordination (Endpoint / DeploymentUnit /
//   sync-async-stream-pubsub semantics, v4-close-interrogation.md §16) —
//   a different concern from intra-program hardware concurrency, which
//   this file demonstrates needs no cross-program coordination carrier
//   at all. Both stale citations are surfaced, not edited: the contract
//   lines are immutable, and the per-file Consumes correction is
//   pending the systemic operator scaffold-Consumes reconciliation
//   (T-4 mgr synthesis msg_1fddb75c) — uniform with ptx #3170, spice
//   #3168, and llvm_ir #3171. The body's `import` reflects what this
//   file actually uses. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
//   ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).


module v4.extdeps.languages.verilog


// std/node.dag is imported: it makes the K-1 opaque `Symbol` (the
// name-reference identity used pervasively in the Verilog grammar —
// module names, port names, signal names, instance names) resolvable
// through this file's import closure. std/algebra.dag is NOT imported:
// this file's scaffold-declared `Inhabitance: bit-vectors per
// std/algebra.dag` is DEFERRED to the operator-pending D2 STOP (see the
// Inhabitance section in modeling notes); declaring per-target
// OrderedRing/Semiring instance-values now would be the per-file
// `<Algebra><Concrete>` improvisation D2 forbids (#3171 worker
// vivid-crab-154 / snappy-badger-570 held precedent). std/diagnostic.dag
// is NOT imported: this declarative LanguageModel declares no
// parse/emit body — those ride the operator-pending D1 Outcome<T>
// carrier (`Produced{value:T} | Rejected{diagnostic:Diagnostic}`) when
// ratified in std/diagnostic.dag; improvising a per-file Result shape
// now is exactly the v4 value-or-Diagnostic STOP first surfaced by
// T-4.6 (keen-wren-419). `Int`, `Bool`, `String`, `List` are
// kernel-ambient per STRUCTURE.md §"Kernel-ambient types" — no import.
import v4.std.node { Symbol }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// SPEC, NOT LIBRARY (M3 / L-2). The anchor is IEEE 1364-2005 — the
// language specification. The set of synthesizable subsets used by any
// particular tool (Xilinx XST, Synopsys Design Compiler, Yosys), the
// vendor-specific timing libraries, and the simulation-only constructs
// (`$display`, `$monitor`, file I/O system tasks) are NOT modeled: an
// invocation of `$display` is just a system-task call (a Symbol-named
// call site); modeling the system-task universe is infinite and the
// wrong layer. SystemVerilog (IEEE 1800) extensions (interfaces,
// always_comb / always_ff / always_latch sugar, packed structs, classes)
// are scope-negative for this file — they are a later language model.
// The 1364-2005 surface is the closed L-2 fidelity authority.
//
// DECLARATIVE MODEL, NOT A RECOGNIZER (T-4 authoring contract). This
// file is the grammar/type/semantics-skeleton as DATA. It declares no
// `parse`/`emit` procedure: those are the GENERIC walker (compiler
// 01_tokenize / 02_parse / 05_emit, B2-OMNI) reading this model. The
// expression sub-grammar (operators, function calls, concatenation,
// system functions) is owned by the bidirectional `LanguageModel`
// substrate that the T-4 bundle introduces — when that shape lands,
// the Verilog statement/expression productions slot into the Verilog
// `LanguageModel` instance; the closed classifiers + Conj records
// declared here are the irreducible structural carrier that does not
// prejudge that shape. Lexical-surface bodies of always-blocks,
// continuous-assign right-hand sides, and control-flow conditions are
// carried as verbatim `String` lexemes at this phase (the spice.dag
// `SpiceValue.lexeme` precedent — `parse ∘ emit` is identity on the
// textual surface; the sub-grammar interprets the lexeme when the
// expression model lands).
//
// IN-B PROBE FINDING (the deliverable — the STOP discipline is the
// product). Verilog HW concurrency models as a plain `LanguageModel`
// over the closed 5 L1 behaviors with NO 6th `Concurrent` / `Parallel`
// behavior and NO core/pipeline change. The probe's stress axis — the
// concurrent semantics of `always` / continuous-assign / module
// instantiation — resolves into ORDINARY Node structure, surfacing
// the right intra-vs-cross-program partition along the way. The
// mapping, member-by-member:
//
//   - MODULE BODY ↦ Conj of parallel siblings.
//     A Verilog `module` body is a flat list of items
//     (`ModuleItem` — continuous-assigns, always-blocks, instances,
//     declarations). Concurrent execution across those siblings is
//     STRUCTURAL: each item is a child of the module-body Conj, and
//     concurrent semantics is the SAME shape as a Conj of submodule
//     instantiations (which every C / Rust / Python module already
//     models as Conj children). The simulator/synthesizer schedule
//     (event-driven evaluation) is the mechanical realization of the
//     dependency graph (THESIS:18 "parallelism is the default —
//     independence is visible in the structure"); it is NOT a
//     separate behavior. Tempting alternative: a `Concurrent` behavior
//     wrapping each module item. REJECTED — it would parallel-
//     represent what the Conj's children already say
//     (feedback_no_annotations: a property structurally visible is
//     never duplicated as a marker). This is precisely the IN-B win.
//
//   - CONTINUOUS ASSIGN (`assign net = expr;`) ↦ Transform.
//     A continuous assignment is a function from the right-hand-side
//     expression's input signals to the left-hand-side net — exactly
//     the Transform behavior (function application, node.dag:209
//     "Transform=application"). The "continuous" aspect — re-evaluate
//     whenever an input changes — is the SCHEDULE of the surrounding
//     dataflow graph, not a property of the Transform itself.
//
//   - ALWAYS BLOCK ↦ Loop (one step) + boundary-driven iteration.
//     Per node.dag A2 footnote (genuine unboundedness — "servers /
//     streams / REPLs is a TERMINATING step iterated by the
//     coordination-boundary driver — never an unbounded `.dag` loop"),
//     each `always @(...)` block's ONE TRIGGER is a BOUNDED step
//     (one event-fire: read inputs, sequence the statements, write
//     outputs). The eternal-iteration ("forever, every clock edge")
//     lives at the simulator/synthesizer's coordination boundary; the
//     `.dag` model carries the BOUNDED step, not the unbounded
//     iteration. The trigger-event-list (`SensitivityItem`s) is the
//     bound that gates the next iteration. Tempting alternative: an
//     unbounded `Eternal` / `Concurrent` behavior. REJECTED — the A2
//     bounded-Loop discipline is the existing surface for this exact
//     pattern.
//
//   - PROCEDURAL STATEMENT SEQUENCE (within `begin ... end`) ↦ Bind.
//     The statements inside an always-block (or initial-block) form
//     a sequential composition — exactly the Bind behavior
//     (node.dag:209 "Bind=let"). Blocking (`=`) vs non-blocking
//     (`<=`) assignment is a SCHEDULING discriminator on the Bind's
//     write semantics (blocking commits before the next statement;
//     non-blocking schedules the write for the end of the time-step),
//     not a new behavior. The `ProceduralAssignKind` enum below
//     carries that discriminator as data on the assignment statement.
//
//   - IF / CASE ↦ Branch.
//     Verilog `if ... else` and `case ... endcase` are guarded sums
//     over a discriminator — exactly the Branch behavior
//     (node.dag:209 "Branch=sums"). The case-item ordering and the
//     full-case / parallel-case synthesis directives are emission-
//     time properties (the synthesizer's concern); the structural
//     surface is the Branch.
//
//   - SIGNAL DECLARATION (Wire / Reg / Vector) ↦ effect-typed value.
//     A Verilog `wire`, `reg`, or `wire [7:0]` is a TYPED signal
//     carrier; the type IS the effect-typing locus per the
//     v4-close-interrogation §16 IN-B disposition ("effects intrinsic
//     to the type signature, NOT an annotation layer"). A continuous-
//     assign Transform's domain/codomain types name Wire/Reg with
//     packing, and the typing is what constrains valid composition
//     (a `reg`-typed driver can drive an `output reg` port; a
//     procedural assignment is only legal to a `reg`-typed
//     destination; etc.). This file's `NetDeclaration` /
//     `VariableDeclaration` / `PortDeclaration` records are the
//     effect-typed carriers; there is no separate Verilog-effect-
//     annotation layer.
//
//   - MODULE INSTANCE ↦ structural Conj child.
//     `ModuleName instance_name (.port(signal), ...);` is a child
//     Node in the parent module-body Conj — the same shape as a
//     submodule reference in any other LanguageModel. Concurrent
//     execution of the instance alongside its sibling items is the
//     same Conj-of-parallel-siblings shape as multiple always-blocks
//     (above); no new behavior.
//
// INTRA-PROGRAM vs CROSS-PROGRAM EFFECT-TYPING (the partition the
// probe surfaces). The scaffold-header `Consumes` line citing
// `extdeps/coordination.dag` for "concurrency via effect-typed Bind
// — IN-B" was a SCAFFOLD-AUTHOR ANTICIPATION that lumped all
// concurrency under one carrier. The probe surfaces the structurally
// correct partition: (a) INTRA-program concurrency (multiple
// `always`-blocks within one Verilog module sharing signal nets) is
// what this file models — the effect-typing carriers are the
// signal-type declarations (`NetType` / `VariableDeclaration` variants /
// `PackingShape`)
// declared HERE, in this language file; (b) CROSS-program coordination
// (one program's output deployed to host A, another to host B,
// communicating via Endpoint / DeploymentUnit / sync-async-stream-
// pubsub) is what `extdeps/coordination.dag` (T-4.8) models — a
// distinct concern with a distinct anchor (Wikipedia Distributed
// computing + Messaging pattern). The IN-B principle "effect-typed
// Bind composition, no 6th behavior" holds in BOTH partitions, with
// the effect-typing carriers in their respective per-language /
// per-coordination home files (P2 single-authority). This is the
// per-language effect-typing finding the probe was authored to
// surface — and it surfaces here on the SUCCESS side, not the STOP
// side.
//
// INHABITANCE (DEFERRED to D2). The scaffold-declared "Inhabitance:
// bit-vectors (wire/reg [N:0]) per std/algebra.dag" is DEFERRED to
// the operator-pending D2 STOP. Conceptually, an N-bit unsigned
// signal grounds in `Semiring<UInt<N>>` (or `OrderedRing<Int<N>>`
// for signed-as-twos-complement reg/integer arithmetic) — but
// declaring a `data verilog_wire_n_semiring: Semiring<UInt<N>> = …`
// instance-value at this layer would be the per-file
// `<Algebra><Concrete>` improvisation D2 was authored to hold
// (PR #3171's hold precedent). The instance-value form lands when
// std/integer.dag / std/logic.dag inhabitance walks land plus the
// ratified D2 instance-value shape; the algebra import + the
// instance-value declarations are added in ONE pass, not improvised
// per language.
//
// PROBE LIMITS (honest framing — what this file does NOT validate).
// The IN-B finding covers the STRUCTURAL fit of Verilog HW concurrency
// to the 5 L1 behaviors. It does NOT validate:
//   - that a synthesizer actually produces gates from `emit`'s output
//     (the "clear win" in the header — that requires invoking a
//     synthesizer process, an `extdeps/process.dag` consumer
//     boundary, not modeled here).
//   - the expression sub-grammar of `assign net = expr;` right-hand
//     sides and procedural statement bodies (verbatim lexemes at
//     this phase; the T-4 LanguageModel bundle owns the recursive
//     expression productions when it lands).
//   - SystemVerilog (IEEE 1800) extensions (`always_comb` /
//     `always_ff` / `always_latch` sugar, interfaces, classes,
//     packed structs) — a later language-version model edits this
//     file (under the L-2 pin discipline) when it lands.
//   - Simulator-only / testbench constructs (`initial` with
//     `$display`, `$finish`, file I/O system tasks, `force` /
//     `release`, fork-join, `wait`) — `initial` is admitted as a
//     `ModuleItem` variant for the once-only-driven case; the
//     `$task` universe is the system-task call site, not the
//     concurrency primitive.
//   - Timing-checks (`$setup`, `$hold`, `specify` blocks) — a
//     separate timing-annotation layer (SDF — Standard Delay
//     Format), out of scope for this language model.
//
// PRACTICE-4 LEDGER DISCIPLINE. Every closed-Disj coproduct below
// carries the full 🟢/🟡/🔴 classification + all FIVE patterns
// attempted (Fact-placement / Variant-is-data / Algebraic-form /
// Dimensional / Parameterized-family-Practice-7) per modeling-
// discipline.md §4 (operator-ratified binding 2026-05-15, gate (b);
// PR #3163 made the 4-pattern miss the proof case — do not omit
// Practice-7). Conj records carry no ledger (only coproducts
// dissolve); scaffold-residual raw-Int fields (VectorRange, VerilogCost)
// carry a 🟡 bridge note mirroring the std/diagnostic.dag /
// ptx Dim3 precedent.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PortDirection — the closed Verilog port-direction classifier
// (IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.2). A module's port is exactly one of three
// directions.
//
//   - Input   — `input`   driven by the instantiating context, read
//                         inside this module
//   - Output  — `output`  driven inside this module, read by the
//                         instantiating context
//   - Inout   — `inout`   bidirectional (tri-state nets only)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A fourth direction is an edit to THIS
// declaration under the L-2 pin discipline (IEEE 1364-2005 has fixed
// three directions; SystemVerilog adds `ref` — a future language-
// version model would EDIT this enum explicitly).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every port consumer (instantiation
//      legality, emission of the `input`/`output`/`inout` keyword,
//      reverse-flow check) reads the direction tag uniformly;
//      shared classifier, not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ name: Symbol }` proxy reopens
//      the readable-string heuristic surface K-1 forecloses (a
//      `match name { "input" => … }` smell). The enum IS the
//      faithful form (algebra.dag:332 `Ordering` precedent).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the three port
//      directions are the Verilog spec's primitive port-flow
//      taxonomy; they do not trace to a richer std/ algebraic
//      structure (no `BoundedLattice<Direction>` with Inout = top —
//      that would be a parallel representation of "Inout admits
//      both directions", a property derived FROM the three tags).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a port has EXACTLY ONE direction;
//      `input output foo` is a syntax error in Verilog itself.
//      Mutually-exclusive alternatives, not simultaneous coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the three are structurally
//      distinct flow-roles (consumer / producer / bidirectional) —
//      NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-
//      declared source set (Practice 7). They are the IEEE 1364-2005
//      spec's irreducible port-flow partition.
// Terminal: the closed three-way port-flow classifier of the
// IEEE 1364-2005 spec; a fourth direction is the C1 stop-signal.
type PortDirection
  = Input
  | Output
  | Inout


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NetType — the closed Verilog net-type classifier (IEEE 1364-2005
// §3.7.1, §5.6). A net carries a value continuously driven by one or
// more drivers; the net-type fixes the resolution function and the
// default value when undriven.
//
//   - Wire     — `wire`     standard net; resolves to X if multiply
//                           driven with conflicting values
//   - Tri      — `tri`      identical to `wire` semantically;
//                           designer-intent marker for tri-state nets
//   - Wand     — `wand`     wired-AND resolution
//   - TriAnd   — `triand`   identical to `wand` semantically;
//                           designer-intent marker pairing with `tri`
//   - Wor      — `wor`      wired-OR resolution
//   - TriOr    — `trior`    identical to `wor` semantically;
//                           designer-intent marker pairing with `tri`
//   - Tri0     — `tri0`     pull-down (resolves to 0 if undriven)
//   - Tri1     — `tri1`     pull-up (resolves to 1 if undriven)
//   - TriReg   — `trireg`   stores last driven value (capacitive)
//   - Supply0  — `supply0`  constant 0 power-rail net
//   - Supply1  — `supply1`  constant 1 power-rail net
//   - Uwire    — `uwire`    unresolved wire — exactly one driver
//                           required (IEEE 1364-2005 §5.6.6)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A new net-type is an edit to THIS
// declaration under the L-2 pin discipline; the IEEE 1364-2005 §3.7.1
// table is the authority.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every net-declaration emitter and
//      every driver-resolution consumer reads the net-type tag
//      uniformly; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ keyword: Symbol }` proxy
//      reopens the readable-string heuristic surface K-1 forecloses
//      (a `match keyword { "wire" => … }` smell); a
//      `{ resolution_kind: Int }` proxy replaces a closed
//      12-valued exhaustive sum with an unbounded Int — both
//      violate P2 + M4 (algebra.dag:332 `Ordering` precedent).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the twelve net-types
//      are the IEEE 1364-2005 spec's primitive resolution-function
//      set; they do not trace to a richer std/ algebraic structure
//      (one could attempt a `Lattice<Resolution>` for the wand/wor
//      sublattice, but that would be a parallel representation of
//      the per-net-type resolution function, not a richer source).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a net declaration carries EXACTLY ONE
//      net-type; `wire wand x` is a syntax error in Verilog itself.
//      Mutually-exclusive alternatives, not coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the twelve are structurally
//      distinct resolution / pull / supply / unresolved variants —
//      NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-
//      declared source set (Practice 7). The Wire/Tri,
//      Wand/TriAnd, Wor/TriOr designer-intent pairs are
//      SEMANTICALLY-equivalent partner variants the spec keeps
//      DISTINCT at the syntactic surface (parse ∘ emit must
//      preserve the writer's intent); collapsing the pairs into
//      `{ resolution: Symbol, tri_intent: Bool }` would be a
//      parallel-representation of the spec's enumeration and lose
//      the K-1-opaque keyword discrimination.
// Terminal: the closed twelve-way net-type classifier of the
// IEEE 1364-2005 spec.
type NetType
  = Wire
  | Tri
  | Wand
  | TriAnd
  | Wor
  | TriOr
  | Tri0
  | Tri1
  | TriReg
  | Supply0
  | Supply1
  | Uwire


// (VariableType has been dissolved into VariableDeclaration's
// Disj-with-named-payloads below — the spec couples kind × packing
// admissibility (only `reg` accepts a `[msb:lsb]` packing per IEEE
// 1364-2005 §5.7), and modeling kind and packing as independent
// coordinates ratified the illegal state "Integer with Packed
// range" — the INVARIANTS P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable anti-
// pattern (codex BLOCKING review on PR #3176). The 5-pattern ledger
// for the dissolved partition lives on VariableDeclaration; the
// reasoning is identical except that "Variant-is-data" now FAILS
// for the additional reason that uniform `{ kind, packing }` ratifies
// the illegal state.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// EdgeTrigger — the closed Verilog sensitivity-event classifier
// (IEEE 1364-2005 §9.7.2). The trigger condition under which an
// always-block's body re-executes when an event-control signal
// transitions.
//
//   - PosEdge    — `posedge` rising edge: 0→1 transition (also
//                            X→1, Z→1; the spec defines edge events
//                            on the implicit ordering 0 < X = Z < 1)
//   - NegEdge    — `negedge` falling edge: 1→0 transition
//   - AnyChange  — bare signal name in the event-list (no edge
//                            keyword): re-executes on ANY signal
//                            transition (level-sensitive)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A fourth edge type is an edit to THIS
// declaration under the L-2 pin discipline (the IEEE 1364-2005 §9.7.2
// edge-event set is the authority).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every always-block's sensitivity-
//      list consumer reads the trigger tag per item (a clock-edge
//      always synthesizes to a flip-flop, a level-sensitive always
//      to combinational or a latch); shared classifier, not
//      scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ direction: Int }` (+1/-1/0)
//      proxy replaces a closed 3-valued exhaustive sum with an
//      unbounded Int — violates P2 + M4 (algebra.dag:332 `Ordering`
//      precedent, which uses the SAME shape).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the three triggers
//      are the IEEE 1364-2005 spec's primitive event-control
//      vocabulary; they do not trace to a richer std/ algebraic
//      structure.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a sensitivity-list item carries
//      EXACTLY ONE trigger; `posedge negedge clk` is a syntax
//      error. Mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the three are structurally
//      distinct event-control shapes (rising-edge / falling-edge /
//      level-sensitive) — NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over
//      a separately-declared source set (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed three-way sensitivity-trigger classifier of
// the IEEE 1364-2005 spec.
type EdgeTrigger
  = PosEdge
  | NegEdge
  | AnyChange


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ProceduralAssignKind — the closed Verilog procedural-assignment
// scheduling discriminator (IEEE 1364-2005 §9.2). Inside an always-
// or initial-block, an assignment to a `reg`-typed destination is
// either BLOCKING or NON-BLOCKING; the discriminator selects when the
// write commits within the simulation time step.
//
//   - Blocking     — `=`  commits BEFORE the next statement in the
//                         Bind chain (sequential-by-construction;
//                         the destination's new value is visible to
//                         the next statement)
//   - NonBlocking  — `<=` SCHEDULES the write for the end of the
//                         time step (the destination's old value is
//                         visible to every subsequent statement in
//                         the same time step; all non-blocking
//                         writes commit at once)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 admits exactly these two
// assignment-scheduling shapes; SystemVerilog's continuous-assign
// inside procedural blocks is a separate construct, not a third
// scheduling shape.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every procedural-assignment emitter
//      AND the synthesis-time race-check ("non-blocking required
//      for flip-flop inference") read the kind uniformly; shared
//      classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ deferred: Bool }` proxy is a
//      faithful 2-valued representation, but the LEXICAL SURFACE
//      (`=` vs `<=`) is a closed two-keyword pair the parser /
//      emitter must dispatch on; the named-enum is the M4-faithful
//      form (closed sets are enums, not Bool proxies — the
//      `Ordering`-as-Int rejection generalizes).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the two are the IEEE
//      1364-2005 spec's primitive scheduling-discriminator pair;
//      they do not trace to a richer std/ algebraic structure.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a procedural assignment carries
//      EXACTLY ONE kind; `x = <= y` is a syntax error.
//      Mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two are structurally
//      distinct scheduling shapes (immediate / deferred) — NOT one
//      generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared
//      source set (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed two-way procedural-assignment scheduling
// classifier of the IEEE 1364-2005 spec.
type ProceduralAssignKind
  = Blocking
  | NonBlocking


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// VectorRange — a packed bit-vector index range `[msb:lsb]`
// (IEEE 1364-2005 §3.10). Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger — only
// coproducts dissolve).
//
//   - msb, lsb     the most- / least-significant bit positions
//
// 🟡 SCAFFOLD (same bridge as std/diagnostic.dag's `Extent.ByteRange`
// and ptx Dim3 raw-Int axes). With only kernel-ambient `Int`
// available at the T-4.9 phase, `{ msb: Int, lsb: Int }`
// STRUCTURALLY ADMITS some states the substrate cannot reject
// (extreme magnitudes far beyond any plausible bus width; degenerate
// gaps the spec doesn't formally forbid in the type system but that
// no synthesizable design uses). PER IEEE 1364-2005 §3.10, msb and
// lsb constant expressions ARE ALLOWED to be positive, negative, or
// zero — the spec explicitly admits `reg [-1:4] b;` as a legal
// example. Negative indices are NOT illegal; the prior scaffold
// bound forbidding them was a misstatement of the spec (openai-pro
// REQUEST_CHANGES on PR #3176 corrected this). The remaining
// scaffold property is that the refined substrate (when it lands)
// can carry per-axis precision and any synthesizable-subset checks
// the consumer tooling needs, while the spec-level value space —
// signed Int for msb/lsb — stays admissible. `[7:0]` is little-
// endian, `[0:7]` is big-endian, `[-1:4]` is signed-msb;
// all legal. It is a TRACKED scaffold, declared with the three
// bridge properties (the precedent diagnostic.dag established
// 2026-05-15):
//   (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; the carrier is provisional. The
//       bounded-Cardinality refinement is the terminal shape; for
//       now the spec's full Int range is the producer-side bound.
//   (b) bounds on use — a producer MUST construct only ranges
//       admitted by IEEE 1364-2005 §3.10 (msb and lsb are any
//       signed Int per spec; negative values are legal); until the
//       substrate enforces additional per-synthesis-tool bounds,
//       those tighter bounds (if any) are producer-side.
//   (c) dissolution trigger — when std/cardinality.dag's refinement
//       substrate lands (T-3, P4 decidability), `msb` / `lsb` become
//       refined signed-Int axes (admitting the spec's full range
//       including negatives), and this scaffold dissolves.
type VectorRange {
  msb: Int
  lsb: Int
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PackingShape — the closed Verilog signal packing classifier. A net
// or variable is either SCALAR (a single bit) or PACKED into a
// bit-vector indexed by a `VectorRange`. IEEE 1364-2005 §5.2.
//
//   - Scalar    — no range declared; the signal is a single bit
//   - Packed    — `[msb:lsb]` packing into a bit-vector
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 admits these two packing
// shapes for nets and variables; multi-dimensional unpacked arrays
// are a separate language-surface concern (§7.4) deferred to a
// future packing-shape extension under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every declaration emitter (net /
//      variable / port) and every signal-width consumer reads the
//      packing shape uniformly; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: `Scalar` is nullary; `Packed`
//      carries a `VectorRange`. Collapsing to `{ width: Int }`
//      replaces a closed two-valued exhaustive sum with an
//      unbounded Int (width=-1 representable) — violates P2 + M4
//      (the illegal-states-unrepresentable shape); also drops the
//      msb/lsb endianness which is a legal Verilog distinction.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the two are the
//      IEEE 1364-2005 spec's primitive packing partition; they do
//      not trace to a richer std/ algebraic structure.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a signal declaration carries EXACTLY
//      ONE packing shape; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: `Scalar` is nullary and
//      `Packed` carries a `VectorRange` — structurally
//      heterogeneous, NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over a
//      separately-declared source set (Practice 7). It IS the
//      irreducible two-way packing partition.
// Terminal: the closed two-way packing classifier; multi-dimensional
// unpacked arrays would be an EDIT to this declaration under the
// L-2 pin discipline (a third variant).
type PackingShape
  = Scalar
  | Packed { range: VectorRange }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Signedness — the closed Verilog net/reg signedness classifier
// (IEEE 1364-2005 §5.6 "Signed types"). The `signed` keyword on a net
// or `reg` declaration selects two's-complement signed interpretation
// of the bit-vector; the default (no keyword) is unsigned. The
// signedness fact governs sign-extension on assignment, comparison
// semantics, and arithmetic operator dispatch — a fact every emission
// / arithmetic consumer must preserve per INVARIANTS P2 (codex
// BLOCKING review on PR #3176 2026-05-16 surfaced this gap; the
// prior NetDeclaration / RegVariable collapsed signed and unsigned
// packed forms, losing a spec-mandated boundary fact).
//
//   - Signed     — `signed` modifier present; two's-complement
//                  signed interpretation; sign-extension on
//                  assignment; signed-arithmetic operator dispatch
//   - Unsigned   — no `signed` modifier; unsigned modular
//                  interpretation; zero-extension on assignment;
//                  unsigned-arithmetic operator dispatch (the spec's
//                  default)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). The signedness axis is BINARY per the
// IEEE 1364-2005 spec; a third interpretation is an edit to THIS
// declaration under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every arithmetic-emission consumer
//      and every assignment-extension consumer reads the signedness
//      tag uniformly to dispatch (signed vs unsigned operator
//      lowering; sign- vs zero-extension); shared classifier, not
//      scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ signed: Bool }` proxy is a
//      faithful 2-valued representation, but the LEXICAL SURFACE
//      (`signed` keyword present / absent) is a closed pair the
//      parser/emitter dispatch on; the named-enum is the M4-faithful
//      form (closed sets are enums, not Bool proxies — same
//      argument as ProceduralAssignKind's ledger).
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each variant grounds in a different
//      std/algebra structure (Signed → OrderedRing on the underlying
//      bit-vector width; Unsigned → Semiring). That grounding is
//      the inhabitance walk DEFERRED to D2 per the file's "Owned
//      ELSEWHERE" framing; the signedness enum itself stays a closed
//      classifier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a net/reg declaration is EXACTLY ONE
//      signedness; `wire signed signed x;` is a syntax error.
//      Mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two are structurally
//      distinct arithmetic-interpretation primitives — NOT one
//      generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared
//      source set (Practice 7). They are the spec's irreducible
//      signedness partition.
// Terminal: the closed two-way Verilog signedness classifier of the
// IEEE 1364-2005 spec.
type Signedness
  = Signed
  | Unsigned


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NetDeclaration — a Verilog net (`wire foo;`, `wire [7:0] bus;`,
// `wire signed [15:0] s;`, `wand x;`). Conj record (no Practice-4
// ledger).
//
//   - net_type     which net-type (wire / wand / wor / tri / …)
//   - name         the net identifier (K-1-opaque Symbol)
//   - packing      scalar or `[msb:lsb]` packed bit-vector
//   - signedness   `signed` keyword present / absent (IEEE 1364-2005
//                  §5.6; the spec admits `signed` on any net-type;
//                  for a scalar (single-bit) net the signedness fact
//                  is semantically vacuous but the spec preserves it
//                  syntactically — parse ∘ emit must preserve the
//                  writer's keyword choice. Added 2026-05-16 per
//                  codex BLOCKING review on PR #3176 to preserve the
//                  IEEE signedness boundary fact for downstream
//                  arithmetic/emission consumers, INVARIANTS P2.)
type NetDeclaration {
  net_type: NetType
  name: Symbol
  packing: PackingShape
  signedness: Signedness
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// VariableDeclaration — a Verilog variable (`reg foo;`, `reg [31:0]
// counter;`, `reg signed [15:0] s;`, `integer i;`, `real x;`,
// `time t;`, `realtime rt;`). IEEE 1364-2005 §3.7.2, §5.7 partitions
// variable declarations into five kinds, where ONLY `reg` admits a
// `[msb:lsb]` packing — the other four (`integer` / `real` / `time`
// / `realtime`) have spec-fixed widths and forbid a vector range
// (`[N:M] integer i;` is a syntax error per §5.7). The kind × packing
// admissibility is modeled STRUCTURALLY: only `RegVariable` carries
// a `packing` payload, so "Integer with Packed range" is
// unrepresentable — not "rejected by a check" — the INVARIANTS P2
// illegal-states-unrepresentable shape (same design choice as
// node.dag's EdgeLabel, rust.dag's RustScalar Disj-with-named-
// payloads, and go.dag's GoScalar). The prior `{ var_type:
// VariableType, packing: PackingShape }` Conj-record-with-
// independent-coordinates form was the codex BLOCKING-review anti-
// pattern on PR #3176; this Disj-with-payloads form is the
// dissolution.
//
// The kind × signedness admissibility is also modeled STRUCTURALLY
// per IEEE 1364-2005 §5.6: `reg` admits `signed` (`reg signed [N:0]`
// is two's-complement signed bit-vector); `integer` is ALWAYS signed
// by spec (§3.7.2: "32-bit signed"); `time` is ALWAYS unsigned (the
// 64-bit simulation-time carrier); `real` and `realtime` are IEEE-754
// floating-point and carry NO signedness keyword (the floating-point
// sign is internal to the IEEE-754 representation, not a separate
// declaration modifier). Only `RegVariable` carries the
// `signedness: Signedness` payload — "RealVariable signedness=Signed"
// is unrepresentable, just like the kind × packing partition (codex
// BLOCKING fix 2026-05-16 on PR #3176; the spec-fixed-signedness
// kinds DO NOT need the carrier because the fact is non-variant per
// spec).
//
//   - RegVariable        — `reg [msb:lsb] name;` / `reg signed [msb:
//                          lsb] name;` / `reg name;` general-purpose
//                          variable; the procedural-assignment
//                          destination; THE ONLY variable-kind that
//                          admits packing AND signedness modifiers
//   - IntegerVariable    — `integer name;` 32-bit signed (spec-fixed
//                          signed); no packing, no signedness payload
//   - RealVariable       — `real name;` double-precision IEEE-754
//                          floating-point; no packing, no signedness
//                          payload (sign is internal to the IEEE-754
//                          representation, not a declaration modifier)
//   - TimeVariable       — `time name;` 64-bit unsigned simulation-
//                          time carrier (spec-fixed unsigned); no
//                          packing, no signedness payload
//   - RealTimeVariable   — `realtime name;` aliased to `real` per
//                          §3.7.2; no packing, no signedness payload
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A new variable-kind is an edit to THIS
// declaration under the L-2 pin discipline (SystemVerilog adds
// `logic` / `bit` / typed enums — a future language-version model
// would EDIT this Disj explicitly).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every variable-declaration emitter,
//      every procedural-assignment legality check (only RegVariable
//      is a legal procedural-assignment destination), every signed-
//      vs-unsigned arithmetic dispatch, and the per-kind algebra
//      inhabitance walk all read the variant tag uniformly; shared
//      classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to `{ kind: VariableType,
//      packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }` ratifies
//      multiple illegal states the spec forbids — "Integer with
//      Packed range" (§5.7) AND "Real with signedness=Signed" (§5.6
//      — real is floating-point, no signedness modifier), AND
//      "Time with signedness=Signed" (spec-fixed unsigned). The
//      Disj-with-heterogeneous-payloads dissolves all of these:
//      only RegVariable carries packing AND signedness; the spec-
//      fixed-attribute kinds carry only `name`. Same dissolution
//      shape as rust.dag's RustScalar and go.dag's GoScalar.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each variant grounds in a std/
//      algebra structure (RegVariable → `Semiring<UInt<N>>` or
//      `OrderedRing<Int<N>>` per declared packing × signedness;
//      IntegerVariable → `OrderedRing<Int<32>>`; RealVariable /
//      RealTimeVariable → `ApproximateField<Float>`; TimeVariable
//      → `Semiring<UInt<64>>`). That grounding is INHABITANCE —
//      owned by the inhabiting carrier file (std/integer.dag,
//      std/float.dag) per P2, and DEFERRED to the operator-pending
//      D2 STOP. The variant set itself stays a closed classifier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single variable declaration IS
//      EXACTLY ONE variant; mutually-exclusive alternatives. The
//      shapes differ across variants (RegVariable carries packing
//      AND signedness; the rest carry neither) — they cannot be
//      coordinates of a single record without the illegal-state
//      smell.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the five are structurally
//      heterogeneous (RegVariable carries packing + signedness; the
//      rest carry neither; the algebraic groundings are five
//      distinct std/algebra structures — Semiring or OrderedRing
//      depending on packing×signedness / OrderedRing<Int<32>> /
//      ApproximateField / Semiring<UInt<64>> / aliased
//      ApproximateField) — NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection
//      over a separately-declared source set (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed five-variant Verilog variable-declaration
// classifier; illegal kind×packing AND kind×signedness combinations
// are unrepresentable by construction.
type VariableDeclaration
  = RegVariable { name: Symbol, packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }
  | IntegerVariable { name: Symbol }
  | RealVariable { name: Symbol }
  | TimeVariable { name: Symbol }
  | RealTimeVariable { name: Symbol }


// (PortDeclarationStyle has been dissolved into ModulePortsList's
// Disj-with-named-payloads at the MODULE level — IEEE 1364-2005
// §12.3.4 forbids mixing ANSI and non-ANSI styles within ONE module,
// so the style-choice belongs on the module's port-list as a whole,
// not as a per-port field. The prior per-port `style: PortDeclaration
// Style` admitted the spec-forbidden mixed-style state (codex BLOCKING
// review on PR #3176 surfaced this — the per-port encoding was a
// product-axis miscarve relative to the spec's module-header
// partition). See ModulePortsList below.)


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PortAnsiTypeKind — the closed Verilog ANSI-port type/kind
// classifier (IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.3, §5.6, §5.7). An ANSI-style port
// declares EITHER a net-type or a variable-kind; spec-admissibility
// of `packing` and `signedness` modifiers DIFFERS per variant — only
// net-types and `reg` admit them, the spec-fixed-width variable kinds
// (integer/real/time/realtime) forbid them. The kind × packing × sign
// admissibility is modeled STRUCTURALLY via Disj-with-heterogeneous-
// payloads: only the variants whose spec form admits packing/sign
// carry those payloads (codex BLOCKING fix on PR #3176 — the prior
// uniform `packing + signedness` on every ANSI-port admitted spec-
// forbidden states like `output integer signed [N:0]`, mirroring
// what the body-side VariableDeclaration Disj already dissolves).
//
//   - AnsiNetType { net_type: NetType, packing: PackingShape,
//                   signedness: Signedness }
//       — `input wire`, `output wand`, `inout tri0`, etc. Net ports
//       admit any net-type from the closed NetType set, any
//       PackingShape, and any signedness (per IEEE 1364-2005 §5.6).
//   - AnsiRegType { packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }
//       — `output reg [N:0]`, `output reg signed`, etc. Reg-typed
//       ANSI ports admit both packing and signedness (§5.6).
//   - AnsiIntegerType
//       — `output integer`. Spec-fixed 32-bit signed (§3.7.2); NO
//       packing, NO signedness modifier.
//   - AnsiRealType
//       — `output real`. IEEE-754 double; NO packing, NO signedness
//       (sign is internal to IEEE-754).
//   - AnsiTimeType
//       — `output time`. Spec-fixed 64-bit unsigned (§3.7.2); NO
//       packing, NO signedness modifier.
//   - AnsiRealTimeType
//       — `output realtime`. Aliased to `real` per §3.7.2; NO
//       packing, NO signedness modifier (parse∘emit preserves the
//       keyword choice).
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.3 admits this six-
// way ANSI-port type/kind partition; a future language-version model
// (SystemVerilog `logic`/`bit`/etc.) would EDIT this Disj explicitly.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: ANSI-port consumers branch on the
//      kind uniformly to dispatch emission / type-check legality /
//      eventual algebra inhabitance; shared classifier, not
//      scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to a uniform `{ kind:
//      Symbol, packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }`
//      record reopens BOTH (a) the readable-string K-1 heuristic
//      surface AND (b) the spec-forbidden illegal states like
//      "AnsiIntegerType with signedness=Signed" that the per-variant
//      payload-presence rule dissolves. Disj-with-heterogeneous-
//      payloads is the dissolved form (P2 + Practice 2/6 + M4) —
//      same shape as body-side VariableDeclaration's kind × packing
//      × signedness dissolution.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each variant grounds in a std/
//      algebra structure (AnsiNetType / AnsiRegType → Semiring or
//      OrderedRing per packing × signedness; AnsiIntegerType →
//      OrderedRing<Int<32>>; AnsiRealType / AnsiRealTimeType →
//      ApproximateField<Float>; AnsiTimeType → Semiring<UInt<64>>);
//      D2-deferred per "Owned ELSEWHERE".
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an ANSI port has EXACTLY ONE type/kind;
//      mutually-exclusive alternatives. The shapes differ across
//      variants (net + reg carry packing+signedness; integer/real/
//      time/realtime carry neither) — they cannot be coordinates of
//      a single record without the illegal-state smell.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the six are structurally
//      heterogeneous in BOTH their payload shapes AND their
//      algebraic groundings (Semiring/OrderedRing depending on
//      packing×signedness / OrderedRing<Int<32>> / ApproximateField
//      / Semiring<UInt<64>> / aliased ApproximateField — five
//      distinct std/algebra groundings plus an additional NetType-
//      payload-bearing variant). NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection
//      over a separately-declared source set (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed six-variant Verilog ANSI-port type/kind
// classifier; illegal kind × packing / kind × signedness combinations
// are unrepresentable by construction.
type PortAnsiTypeKind
  = AnsiNetType { net_type: NetType, packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }
  | AnsiRegType { packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }
  | AnsiIntegerType
  | AnsiRealType
  | AnsiTimeType
  | AnsiRealTimeType


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// OutputPortAnsiTypeKind — the closed Verilog ANSI-OUTPUT-port
// type/kind classifier. IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.3 admits ALL six
// PortAnsiTypeKind variants for outputs MINUS real / realtime (the
// spec explicitly states "An output port can be of any net or
// variable type EXCEPT a real type" — floating-point types are not
// admitted as output ports because they have no well-defined
// inter-module wire representation). The OUTPUT-specific subset is
// modeled here; the spec-illegal AnsiRealType / AnsiRealTimeType
// outputs are unrepresentable by construction (codex BLOCKING fix on
// PR #3176 2026-05-16 — the prior AnsiOutputPort reused
// PortAnsiTypeKind directly, admitting `output real` / `output
// realtime` which the spec forbids).
//
//   - OutputAnsiNetType { net_type: NetType, packing: PackingShape,
//                         signedness: Signedness }
//       — `output wire [7:0] data`; net family.
//   - OutputAnsiRegType { packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }
//       — `output reg [7:0] count`; reg-typed (procedural-assignable
//       output, the most common parameterized output form).
//   - OutputAnsiIntegerType
//       — `output integer counter`; 32-bit signed.
//   - OutputAnsiTimeType
//       — `output time t`; 64-bit unsigned simulation-time.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.3 admits exactly
// this four-way output-type partition; SystemVerilog adds further
// types — future language-version model EDITS this Disj explicitly.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every output-port consumer reads the
//      kind to dispatch emission / type-check legality; shared
//      classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: reusing PortAnsiTypeKind directly
//      (the prior shape) admits AnsiRealType / AnsiRealTimeType
//      outputs — exactly the §12.3.3 illegal state this Disj
//      dissolves. P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each variant grounds in a std/
//      algebra structure (OutputAnsiNetType / OutputAnsiRegType →
//      Semiring or OrderedRing per packing × signedness;
//      OutputAnsiIntegerType → OrderedRing<Int<32>>;
//      OutputAnsiTimeType → Semiring<UInt<64>>). D2-deferred per
//      "Owned ELSEWHERE".
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an ANSI output port has EXACTLY ONE
//      type/kind; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — PARTIAL: this Disj is a SUBSET
//      projection of PortAnsiTypeKind (the 6-variant general
//      partition minus real/realtime). The projection is the spec's
//      output-port-admissibility filter — not a generic `F<X>` over
//      a richer source set (Practice 7), because the real/realtime
//      exclusion IS the partition's defining filter.
// Terminal: the closed four-way Verilog ANSI-output-port type/kind
// classifier; real/realtime outputs unrepresentable by construction
// per IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.3.
type OutputPortAnsiTypeKind
  = OutputAnsiNetType { net_type: NetType, packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }
  | OutputAnsiRegType { packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }
  | OutputAnsiIntegerType
  | OutputAnsiTimeType


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NonAnsiPort — a non-ANSI-style Verilog module-HEADER port entry
// (`module M (a, b, c);` — the header lists port-EXPRESSIONS only;
// direction lives in BODY port_declaration items per IEEE 1364-2005
// §12.3.2). Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - port_expression_lexeme   the port_expression as a verbatim
//                              lexeme (per spec grammar the
//                              port_expression can be a bare
//                              identifier `a`, a bit/part-select
//                              `a[3:0]`, a concatenation `{a, b[3:0]}`,
//                              or a renamed-port form
//                              `.external_name(internal_expression)`).
//                              Sub-grammar deferred to the T-4
//                              LanguageModel via the established
//                              body_lexeme precedent — at the
//                              vocabulary-slice layer the slice
//                              carries the port-expression text
//                              losslessly; downstream
//                              port-binding resolution is the
//                              LanguageModel grammar's concern.
//
// Per IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.2 the non-ANSI header carries port-
// expressions ONLY — port direction (input/output/inout) and any
// inline type/packing/signedness facts live in BODY
// `port_declaration` items (see PortDeclaration below + the
// PortDeclarationItem ModuleItem variant). The prior
// `PortDeclaration { direction, name }` on the header port-list
// collapsed this spec-boundary; corrected per codex BLOCKING on
// PR #3176 2026-05-16.
type NonAnsiPort {
  port_expression_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PortDeclaration — a non-ANSI-style Verilog BODY port_declaration
// (`input a;`, `output b;`, `inout c;`). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.4 +
// §12.2 admit these as module-body items. Each declares port
// direction + name; the type/packing/signedness facts come from a
// MATCHING `NetDeclaration` / `VariableDeclaration` in the same
// module body (the non-ANSI separated form), OR — per spec — a body
// port_declaration may combine direction + type inline (`input wire
// [7:0] a;`). The combined form is structurally equivalent to ANSI
// and is admitted by the same `AnsiPortDeclaration` carrier surfaced
// as `BodyPortDeclarationItem` in ModuleItem; this PortDeclaration
// record covers the direction-only form.
//
//   - direction  input / output / inout
//   - name       the port identifier (K-1-opaque Symbol; resolves
//                through the namespace to the matching body
//                Net/Variable declaration that carries the type-
//                fact, when separated)
//
// Per IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.4 / §12.2 PortDeclaration is a MODULE
// BODY item (PortDeclarationItem in ModuleItem), NOT a module-
// header element. The non-ANSI header carries port-EXPRESSIONS via
// NonAnsiPort above; this body declaration carries the direction
// fact that the non-ANSI form requires. Corrected per codex
// BLOCKING on PR #3176 2026-05-16 — the prior shape mis-located
// PortDeclaration on the header port-list, conflating the spec's
// header-vs-body partition.
type PortDeclaration {
  direction: PortDirection
  name: Symbol
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// AnsiNetPortType — the closed type/payload variants admitted for an
// ANSI port that ONLY accepts net types (input + inout ports per IEEE
// 1364-2005 §12.3.3). Input and inout ports cannot be reg-typed
// (procedural assignment requires reg, which only output can carry),
// and cannot be the spec-fixed-width variable kinds (integer / real /
// time / realtime are output-only per spec). This carrier is the
// closed type-payload set for input / inout — strictly the net family.
//
//   - AnsiInputInoutNet { net_type: NetType, packing: PackingShape,
//                         signedness: Signedness }
//       — any NetType variant (wire/wand/wor/tri/etc.) with optional
//       packing + signedness per spec §5.6.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.3 admits exactly net
// types for input/inout ANSI ports; SystemVerilog `logic` would EDIT
// this Disj explicitly.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Single-variant Disj (currently exactly one
// type-shape — net with payload — admitted; future spec extensions
// would add variants). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every input/inout-ANSI consumer reads
//      the type uniformly to dispatch emission / drive net-resolution;
//      shared classifier.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the single variant carries a
//      structured payload (net_type + packing + signedness); a
//      uniform record would collapse the future-extension axis a
//      Disj preserves.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: net types are the spec's
//      primitive resolution-function set per §3.7.1; D2-deferred per
//      "Owned ELSEWHERE".
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an input/inout ANSI port has EXACTLY ONE
//      net-type payload; the variant is the dispatcher.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the single variant's payload is
//      a structured record (NetType × PackingShape × Signedness),
//      not one generic `F<X>` projection (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed net-only type-payload classifier for input /
// inout ANSI ports; a second variant would be an L-2 pin edit.
type AnsiNetPortType
  = AnsiInputInoutNet { net_type: NetType, packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// AnsiPortDeclaration — an ANSI-style Verilog module port (`module M
// (input wire [7:0] a, output reg b, output reg signed [3:0] count,
// inout tri bus, …);`). The header carries direction, name, AND a
// direction-specific type payload. Per IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.3 the
// port declaration is "completely declared" by this single carrier;
// a body redeclaration is a SYNTAX ERROR.
//
// Direction × type-kind admissibility is modeled STRUCTURALLY via
// Disj-with-named-payloads per direction (codex BLOCKING fix on
// PR #3176 2026-05-16: the prior uniform `{ direction, name, type_
// kind }` Conj record admitted spec-forbidden combinations like
// `input reg [N:0]` and `inout integer` — INVARIANTS P2 illegal-
// states-unrepresentable violation. Per spec §12.3.3 input/inout
// ports MUST be net-typed; only output ports admit reg/integer/real/
// time/realtime variable kinds. The per-direction split makes those
// illegal combinations structurally unrepresentable, mirroring the
// VariableDeclaration kind×packing×signedness dissolution).
//
//   - AnsiInputPort { name: Symbol, net: AnsiNetPortType }
//       — input ports are net-typed only (the spec forbids
//       `input reg`).
//   - AnsiOutputPort { name: Symbol, type_kind: PortAnsiTypeKind }
//       — output ports admit the full PortAnsiTypeKind set (net or
//       any variable kind; output is the only direction that
//       admits reg/integer/real/time/realtime).
//   - AnsiInoutPort { name: Symbol, net: AnsiNetPortType }
//       — inout ports are net-typed only (bidirectional requires
//       nets; `inout reg` is a spec violation).
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.3 admits exactly
// these three direction-bearing ANSI-port forms; SystemVerilog
// `ref` ports would EDIT this Disj explicitly.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every ANSI-port consumer branches on
//      direction × type-kind to dispatch emission / type-check
//      legality / drive net-vs-reg-assignment-legality; shared
//      classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to a uniform
//      `{ direction: PortDirection, name: Symbol, type_kind:
//      PortAnsiTypeKind }` ratifies spec-forbidden combinations
//      (Input + AnsiRegType, Inout + AnsiIntegerType, etc.) — the
//      product-axis admits illegal states this per-direction Disj
//      dissolves. INVARIANTS P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the three direction-
//      bearing ANSI forms are the spec's primitive direction × type
//      admissibility table — not a richer std/ algebraic structure.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single ANSI port is EXACTLY ONE
//      direction variant; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: AnsiInputPort and AnsiInoutPort
//      carry `net: AnsiNetPortType` (net-only); AnsiOutputPort
//      carries `type_kind: PortAnsiTypeKind` (full type-kind set
//      including reg/integer/real/time/realtime). The payload
//      types differ structurally — NOT one generic `F<direction>` /
//      projection over a single source set (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed three-way Verilog ANSI-port direction-kind
// classifier; illegal direction × type-kind combinations are
// unrepresentable by construction.
type AnsiPortDeclaration
  = AnsiInputPort { name: Symbol, net: AnsiNetPortType }
  | AnsiOutputPort { name: Symbol, type_kind: OutputPortAnsiTypeKind }
  | AnsiInoutPort { name: Symbol, net: AnsiNetPortType }


// (The standalone ModulePortsList Disj has been dissolved into
// VerilogModule's own per-style Disj — VerilogModule is now
// NonAnsiVerilogModule | AnsiVerilogModule, each variant carrying its
// own header-port list type AND its own admissible body-items list
// type. This lifts the port-style choice from the port-list carrier
// to the module-as-a-whole carrier, making "ANSI port-list module
// with body PortDeclarationItem" structurally unrepresentable —
// INVARIANTS P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable fix per codex BLOCKING
// on PR #3176 2026-05-16. The §12.3.4 module-style partition is now
// the dimensional axis of VerilogModule itself; the rationale
// formerly housed here is folded into VerilogModule's ledger below.)


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ParameterDeclaration — a Verilog compile-time constant
// (`parameter WIDTH = 8;`). Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - name              the parameter identifier (K-1-opaque Symbol)
//   - default_lexeme    the default value as a verbatim lexeme
//                       (sub-grammar deferred to the T-4 LanguageModel
//                       expression productions; the spice.dag
//                       `SpiceValue.lexeme` precedent — `parse ∘ emit`
//                       is identity on the textual surface)
type ParameterDeclaration {
  name: Symbol
  default_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SensitivityItem — one entry in an always-block's event-control list
// (`posedge clk`, `negedge rst`, `posedge (a & b)`, bare expression).
// IEEE 1364-2005 §9.7.2. Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - trigger                   which edge / level-sensitivity
//   - event_expression_lexeme   the expression the trigger watches
//                               as a verbatim lexeme. Per spec
//                               §9.7.2 the event_expression can be
//                               ANY expression (identifier, bit-
//                               select, arithmetic combination), not
//                               just a bare identifier — corrected
//                               2026-05-16 per codex BLOCKING on
//                               PR #3176 (the prior `signal: Symbol`
//                               narrowed event expressions to
//                               identifier references, excluding
//                               valid syntax). Sub-grammar deferred
//                               to the T-4 LanguageModel via the
//                               established body_lexeme precedent.
type SensitivityItem {
  trigger: EdgeTrigger
  event_expression_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ContinuousAssign — a Verilog continuous-assignment statement
// (`assign net = expr;`, `assign {a, b} = c;`, `assign x[3:0] = …`).
// IEEE 1364-2005 §6.1. Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - lhs_lexeme       the destination net_lvalue as a verbatim
//                      lexeme. Per spec §6.1 the lvalue of a
//                      continuous assign is a net_lvalue: an
//                      identifier reference, a net bit-select, a
//                      net part-select, or a concatenation/replication
//                      of net_lvalues — NOT just a bare identifier
//                      (corrected 2026-05-16 per codex BLOCKING on
//                      PR #3176; the prior `lhs: Symbol` narrowed
//                      lvalues to identifier references, excluding
//                      valid §6.1 net_lvalue forms). Sub-grammar
//                      deferred to the T-4 LanguageModel. Procedural
//                      variables cannot be continuous-assignment
//                      destinations — that joint check is the T-4
//                      LanguageModel's concern.
//   - rhs_lexeme       the right-hand-side expression as a verbatim
//                      lexeme (sub-grammar deferred — same shape as
//                      `ParameterDeclaration.default_lexeme`)
type ContinuousAssign {
  lhs_lexeme: String
  rhs_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// AlwaysBlock — a Verilog `always @(...)` block (IEEE 1364-2005 §9.9).
// Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - sensitivity    the event-control specification (closed Disj
//                    over the three IEEE 1364-2005 §9.7.2 forms:
//                    no event control / implicit `@*` / explicit
//                    event list)
//   - body_lexeme    the procedural body (begin…end or single
//                    statement) as a verbatim lexeme (sub-grammar
//                    deferred to the T-4 LanguageModel expression /
//                    statement productions; `parse ∘ emit` is
//                    identity on the textual surface)
//
// Per the IN-B PROBE FINDING above, this carrier IS the Loop-step +
// Bind-chain unit: the always-block's ONE TRIGGER is a bounded step
// (A2 footnote); the iteration across triggers is the simulator/
// synthesizer's coordination boundary; the sequential composition of
// statements inside the body is the Bind chain.
type AlwaysBlock {
  sensitivity: AlwaysBlockSensitivity
  body_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// AlwaysBlockSensitivity — the closed Verilog event-control
// classifier for an `always` block (IEEE 1364-2005 §9.7.2). The spec
// admits exactly THREE event-control forms; modeling sensitivity as
// `List<SensitivityItem>` collapsed the implicit `@*` form with both
// "no event control" (empty list) and "explicit event list" (non-
// empty) — the codex BLOCKING review on PR #3176 surfaced the gap.
// This Disj-with-named-payloads makes each form structurally distinct
// per INVARIANTS P1 modeling-faithfulness + P2 single-authority.
//
//   - NoEventControl         — bare `always begin ... end` with no
//                              `@(...)` — the body uses internal
//                              delay control (`#10 ...`) for timing.
//                              Rare in practice; legal per spec.
//   - ImplicitAtStar         — `always @*` or `always @(*)` —
//                              implicit sensitivity list covering
//                              ALL signals read in the body (the
//                              §9.7.2 implicit-event-control form;
//                              recommended for combinational logic
//                              to avoid manual list errors). The
//                              implicit set is COMPUTED FROM THE
//                              BODY at synthesis / simulation time;
//                              this variant carries no payload —
//                              the body itself is the authority for
//                              which signals trigger the always.
//   - ExplicitEventList      — `always @(event_expression)` —
//                              explicit list of edge-triggered or
//                              level-sensitive entries (`posedge
//                              clk, negedge rst, data`). The
//                              `items` payload is the list of
//                              `SensitivityItem` entries.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 §9.7.2 admits exactly
// these three event-control forms; a fourth would be a future
// language-version change requiring edit under the L-2 pin
// discipline.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every always-block consumer (the
//      synthesis dispatch for flip-flop vs latch vs combinational
//      inference, the implicit-set-from-body extraction, the
//      explicit-list emission) branches on the form uniformly;
//      shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to
//      `{ items: List<SensitivityItem> }` (the prior model)
//      conflates NoEventControl (empty list) with ExplicitEventList
//      (empty list is technically possible) AND has NO
//      representation for ImplicitAtStar — exactly the codex
//      BLOCKING finding. The Disj-with-heterogeneous-payloads is
//      the dissolution: each variant carries exactly the structural
//      data its form admits.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the three forms are
//      the IEEE 1364-2005 spec's primitive event-control partition;
//      they do not trace to a richer std/ algebraic structure.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single `always` block has EXACTLY
//      ONE event-control form; mutually-exclusive alternatives
//      (per spec: a single `always` cannot have both an implicit
//      and explicit list).
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the three variants carry
//      structurally heterogeneous payloads (NoEventControl is
//      nullary; ImplicitAtStar is a nullary marker — the implicit
//      set is computed from the body, not stored; ExplicitEventList
//      carries a `List<SensitivityItem>`) — NOT one generic `F<X>`
//      / projection over a separately-declared source set
//      (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed three-way Verilog always-block event-control
// classifier of the IEEE 1364-2005 spec.
type AlwaysBlockSensitivity
  = NoEventControl
  | ImplicitAtStar
  | ExplicitEventList { items: List<SensitivityItem> }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// InitialBlock — a Verilog `initial` block (IEEE 1364-2005 §9.9).
// Executes its body ONCE at simulation time 0. Conj record (no
// Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - body_lexeme    the procedural body as a verbatim lexeme (sub-
//                    grammar deferred — same shape as `AlwaysBlock.
//                    body_lexeme`)
//
// `initial` is admitted here for the simulation-time-zero
// initialization case (the synthesizable-subset uses register-reset
// in `always @(posedge clk)` blocks instead); per the PROBE LIMITS
// header note the testbench-only `$display` / `$finish` system tasks
// inside `initial` are out of scope.
type InitialBlock {
  body_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PortConnectionActual — the closed Verilog port-connection-actual
// classifier (IEEE 1364-2005 §12.1.2). The spec admits a port-
// connection's actual to be any of three forms: a full expression
// (including identifier references, literals, bit-selects, arithmetic
// — `.en(1'b1)`, `.data(bus[3:0])`, `.sum(a + b)`), or BLANK (an
// explicitly unconnected port — `.unused()`). Modeling the actual as
// a bare `Symbol` (the prior form) narrowed it to identifier
// references only, excluding valid IEEE 1364-2005 syntax — openai-pro
// REQUEST_CHANGES on PR #3176 surfaced this gap. INVARIANTS P1
// modeling-faithfulness violation (the spec's port-connection-actual
// partition is wider than just identifiers).
//
//   - ExpressionConnection { expression_lexeme: String }
//       — the connection-actual is a Verilog expression carried as
//       a verbatim lexeme (sub-grammar deferred to the T-4
//       LanguageModel grammar productions; the spice.dag
//       `SpiceValue.lexeme` precedent — `parse ∘ emit` is identity
//       on the textual surface; the expression-typed-fact lands
//       when the bundled expression productions land). Covers
//       identifier references (`.en(clk)`), literals (`.en(1'b1)`),
//       bit-selects (`.data(bus[3:0])`), and arithmetic
//       (`.sum(a + b)`).
//   - BlankConnection
//       — `.port_name()` — an explicitly unconnected port (spec
//       §12.1.2 admits this form for documenting that a named port
//       is intentionally left unconnected); nullary marker.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.1.2 admits these two
// actual-forms; a third would be an edit to THIS declaration under
// the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every port-connection emitter and
//      every dependency-graph walker reads the actual form
//      uniformly (a blank connection contributes no dependency; a
//      non-blank actual contributes the expression's signal
//      references); shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to `{ expression_lexeme:
//      String }` where blank = empty-string conflates "intentionally
//      blank" with "expression is empty string" (the lexeme `""` is
//      not a valid Verilog expression); the Disj-with-named-payloads
//      makes blank vs expression structurally distinct.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the two actual-forms
//      are the IEEE 1364-2005 spec's port-connection-actual partition;
//      they do not trace to a richer std/ algebraic structure.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single port-connection actual is
//      EXACTLY ONE form; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: ExpressionConnection carries a
//      lexeme payload; BlankConnection is nullary — structurally
//      heterogeneous, NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over a
//      separately-declared source set (Practice 7). The expression
//      sub-grammar is owned by the bundled T-4 LanguageModel; this
//      vocabulary slice declares the actual-form partition only.
// Terminal: the closed two-way Verilog port-connection-actual
// classifier; the expression sub-grammar is deferred to T-4
// LanguageModel.
type PortConnectionActual
  = ExpressionConnection { expression_lexeme: String }
  | BlankConnection


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NamedPortConnection — one named connection in a module-instance's
// port-binding list (IEEE 1364-2005 §12.1.2, named form). Conj record
// (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - port      the formal port name on the instantiated module
//               (K-1-opaque Symbol; resolved through the namespace
//               to the matching `PortDeclaration` on the target
//               module)
//   - actual    the connection's actual value — an expression
//               (carried as a verbatim lexeme per the closed
//               PortConnectionActual classifier above) or a blank
//               `.port_name()` marker. The prior `signal: Symbol`
//               narrowed this to identifier references only —
//               corrected per openai-pro REQUEST_CHANGES on
//               PR #3176 to admit the full spec-mandated set of
//               expression actuals + blank.
type NamedPortConnection {
  port: Symbol
  actual: PortConnectionActual
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PortConnections — the closed Verilog port-binding-list form
// classifier (IEEE 1364-2005 §12.1.2). The spec admits exactly TWO
// port-connection forms; each module-instance picks ONE form for the
// entire instance (mixing positional and named in a single instance
// is forbidden by the grammar — `Adder a1 (x, .b(y))` is a syntax
// error). The Disj-with-named-payloads makes "mixed positional/named"
// structurally unrepresentable (codex BLOCKING fix on PR #3176
// 2026-05-16: the prior NAMED-ONLY model had positional connections
// scope-negative, which excluded valid spec syntax and violated
// INVARIANTS P1 modeling-faithfulness).
//
//   - PositionalConnections   — `Adder a1 (sig1, sig2, sig3)` —
//                               actuals listed in declaration-order
//                               position; the i-th signal binds to
//                               the i-th port of the instantiated
//                               module. Spec §12.1.2 "Ordered port
//                               connections".
//   - NamedConnections         — `Adder a1 (.a(x), .b(y), .sum(z))`
//                               — actuals explicitly bound to formals
//                               by name. Spec §12.1.2 "Named port
//                               connections".
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 admits exactly these two
// port-connection forms; a third would be an edit to THIS declaration
// under the L-2 pin discipline (SystemVerilog adds `.port` shorthand
// for `.port(port)` and `.*` for "connect all matching names" — a
// future language-version model would EDIT this Disj explicitly).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every instance-emission consumer and
//      every port-binding resolver reads the connection form
//      uniformly to know whether to align by position or by name;
//      shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing the variants to a uniform
//      `List<PortConnectionEntry>` where each entry has `kind: Form`
//      ratifies the illegal state "mixed positional+named within
//      one instance" — exactly the spec-forbidden form. The
//      Disj-with-heterogeneous-payloads makes the per-instance
//      uniform-form choice structural.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the two forms are the
//      IEEE 1364-2005 spec's primitive port-connection partition;
//      they do not trace to a richer std/ algebraic structure.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single module instance is EXACTLY
//      ONE form; mutually-exclusive alternatives. (Mixed-form is a
//      syntax error per spec.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two variants carry
//      structurally heterogeneous payloads (PositionalConnections
//      carries `List<PortConnectionActual>` — the actuals by
//      position; NamedConnections carries
//      `List<NamedPortConnection>` — each with formal+actual) —
//      NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-
//      declared source set (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed two-way Verilog port-connection-form
// classifier of the IEEE 1364-2005 spec.
type PortConnections
  = PositionalConnections { actuals: List<PortConnectionActual> }
  | NamedConnections { connections: List<NamedPortConnection> }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NamedParameterOverride — one named parameter override on a module
// instantiation (`.WIDTH(8)`). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.1.3. Conj record
// (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - parameter    the formal parameter name on the instantiated
//                  module (K-1-opaque Symbol; resolves to a matching
//                  ParameterDeclaration on the target module)
//   - actual       the override value as a verbatim lexeme (expression
//                  sub-grammar deferred to the T-4 LanguageModel —
//                  same precedent as PortConnectionActual /
//                  ParameterDeclaration.default_lexeme)
type NamedParameterOverride {
  parameter: Symbol
  actual: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ParameterOverrides — the closed Verilog module-instance parameter-
// override classifier (IEEE 1364-2005 §12.1.3). A module
// instantiation can override the instantiated module's parameter
// declarations via the `#(...)` syntax (`Adder #(.WIDTH(8)) a1
// (.a(x), .b(y))` named form, or `Adder #(8) a1 (x, y)` ordered
// form), or omit overrides entirely. The spec admits three mutually-
// exclusive forms; modeling parameter-overrides as omitted (the prior
// ModuleInstance shape) excluded valid Verilog syntax that
// parameterized instantiations depend on (codex BLOCKING review on
// PR #3176). INVARIANTS P1 modeling-faithfulness violation.
//
//   - NoParameterOverrides
//       — `Adder a1 (.a(x), .b(y))` — no `#(...)` clause; the
//       instantiated module uses its declared default-parameter
//       values.
//   - OrderedParameterOverrides { actuals: List<String> }
//       — `Adder #(8, 16) a1 (...)` — overrides listed in
//       declaration-order position; the i-th actual overrides the
//       i-th parameter of the instantiated module. Each actual is a
//       verbatim expression lexeme.
//   - NamedParameterOverrides { overrides: List<NamedParameterOverride> }
//       — `Adder #(.WIDTH(8), .DEPTH(16)) a1 (...)` — overrides
//       explicitly bound to formals by name.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.1.3 admits exactly
// these three parameter-override forms; mixing ordered and named
// within one `#(...)` clause is a syntax error per spec — the Disj-
// with-named-payloads makes that uniform-form choice structural
// (same shape as PortConnections).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every instance-emission consumer and
//      every parameter-resolution walker reads the override form
//      uniformly; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing the three forms to a
//      uniform `{ named?: List<...>, ordered?: List<...> }` ratifies
//      "both named and ordered in one clause" — exactly the spec-
//      forbidden mixed form. The Disj makes per-instance uniformity
//      structural.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the three forms are
//      the IEEE 1364-2005 spec's parameter-override partition; they
//      do not trace to a richer std/ algebraic structure.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single module instance has EXACTLY
//      ONE parameter-override form; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: NoParameterOverrides is
//      nullary; OrderedParameterOverrides carries
//      `List<String>` (verbatim expression lexemes by position);
//      NamedParameterOverrides carries `List<NamedParameterOverride>`
//      (each with formal + actual) — structurally heterogeneous,
//      NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over a single source set
//      (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed three-way Verilog module-instance parameter-
// override classifier of the IEEE 1364-2005 spec.
type ParameterOverrides
  = NoParameterOverrides
  | OrderedParameterOverrides { actuals: List<String> }
  | NamedParameterOverrides { overrides: List<NamedParameterOverride> }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ModuleInstance — a Verilog module instantiation (`Adder a1 (.a(x),
// .b(y), .sum(z));` or `Adder a1 (x, y, sum);` or `Adder #(.WIDTH(8))
// a1 (...)`). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.1. Conj record (no Practice-4
// ledger).
//
//   - module_name           the instantiated module's name (K-1-
//                           opaque Symbol; resolved through the
//                           namespace to the matching top-level
//                           `VerilogModule`)
//   - instance_name         this instance's local name (K-1-opaque
//                           Symbol)
//   - parameter_overrides   the `#(...)` parameter-override clause —
//                           none, ordered, or named per spec §12.1.3
//                           (corrected per codex BLOCKING on
//                           PR #3176: the prior ModuleInstance had
//                           NO parameter-override carrier, omitting
//                           a §12.1 spec construct).
//   - connections           the port-binding list (PositionalConnections
//                           OR NamedConnections per spec §12.1.2;
//                           uniform per instance)
type ModuleInstance {
  module_name: Symbol
  instance_name: Symbol
  parameter_overrides: ParameterOverrides
  connections: PortConnections
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// DefparamDeclaration — a Verilog `defparam` statement
// (`defparam top.sub.WIDTH = 8;`). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.2 +
// §4.10.2.1. Deprecated per spec but admitted. Conj record (no
// Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - target_path     the hierarchical target-parameter path as a
//                     verbatim lexeme (sub-grammar deferred to the
//                     T-4 LanguageModel — hierarchical identifier
//                     resolution is its concern)
//   - value_lexeme    the override value as a verbatim expression
//                     lexeme (same body_lexeme precedent as
//                     ParameterDeclaration.default_lexeme)
type DefparamDeclaration {
  target_path: String
  value_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GenerateRegion — a Verilog `generate ... endgenerate` block
// (IEEE 1364-2005 §12.4, §12.1.3.3). Generate regions admit
// `generate-for`, `generate-if`, and `generate-case` constructs that
// are ELABORATED at compile time to produce additional module items.
// Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - body_lexeme    the entire `generate ... endgenerate` body as
//                    a verbatim lexeme (sub-grammar deferred to the
//                    T-4 LanguageModel — the elaboration rules for
//                    generate-for / generate-if / generate-case +
//                    the elaboration-time iteration are owned by
//                    the bundled LanguageModel productions when
//                    they land)
type GenerateRegion {
  body_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SpecifyBlock — a Verilog `specify ... endspecify` block
// (IEEE 1364-2005 §14, §12.2). Specify blocks declare module path
// delays + timing checks (`$setup`, `$hold`, `$width`, etc.). Conj
// record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - body_lexeme    the entire `specify ... endspecify` body as a
//                    verbatim lexeme (sub-grammar deferred — the
//                    SDF-class timing-check vocabulary is owned by
//                    the bundled T-4 LanguageModel productions when
//                    they land; per the header PROBE LIMITS,
//                    timing-checks are a separate layer)
type SpecifyBlock {
  body_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TaskDeclaration — a Verilog `task ... endtask` declaration
// (IEEE 1364-2005 §10.2, §12.2). A task is a procedural sub-routine
// callable from procedural code; it can carry input/output/inout
// arguments and contain delay/event controls (unlike `function`).
// Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - name           the task identifier (K-1-opaque Symbol)
//   - body_lexeme    the procedural body (port list + statements
//                    between `task name;` and `endtask`) as a
//                    verbatim lexeme (sub-grammar deferred to the
//                    T-4 LanguageModel)
type TaskDeclaration {
  name: Symbol
  body_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FunctionDeclaration — a Verilog `function ... endfunction`
// declaration (IEEE 1364-2005 §10.3, §12.2). A function is a pure
// (synthesizable) sub-routine that returns a value; the spec
// forbids delay/event controls inside the body (distinguishing
// functions from tasks). Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - name           the function identifier (K-1-opaque Symbol)
//   - body_lexeme    the function body (return-type + arguments +
//                    statements between `function name;` and
//                    `endfunction`) as a verbatim lexeme (sub-
//                    grammar deferred to the T-4 LanguageModel —
//                    the return-type declaration + the pure-body
//                    constraint enforcement live there)
type FunctionDeclaration {
  name: Symbol
  body_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// EventDeclaration — a Verilog named-event declaration (`event ev;`).
// IEEE 1364-2005 §9.7.3 / §10.3. Named events are explicitly-
// triggered control-flow primitives (`-> ev;` triggers, `@ev` waits).
// Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - name    the event identifier (K-1-opaque Symbol)
type EventDeclaration {
  name: Symbol
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GenvarDeclaration — a Verilog `genvar` declaration (`genvar i;`).
// IEEE 1364-2005 §12.4.2. Generate variables drive elaboration-time
// loop iteration in `generate-for` constructs. Conj record (no
// Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - name    the genvar identifier (K-1-opaque Symbol)
type GenvarDeclaration {
  name: Symbol
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// LocalParameterDeclaration — a Verilog `localparam` declaration
// (`localparam W = 8;`). IEEE 1364-2005 §3.10, §12.2. Local parameters
// differ from ordinary parameters in that they CANNOT be overridden
// by `defparam` or by parameter overrides on a module instantiation
// (the spec's distinct binding rule). Conj record (no Practice-4
// ledger).
//
//   - name              the localparam identifier (K-1-opaque Symbol)
//   - default_lexeme    the value as a verbatim expression lexeme
//                       (mirrors ParameterDeclaration; sub-grammar
//                       deferred)
type LocalParameterDeclaration {
  name: Symbol
  default_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PrimitiveGateKind — the closed Verilog built-in primitive-gate
// kind classifier (IEEE 1364-2005 §7.2). The spec admits exactly 26
// primitive gate types partitioned into six families per §7. Modeling
// the keyword as a free `String` admits spec-illegal keywords —
// corrected per codex BLOCKING on PR #3176 (M4 closed-sets-are-enums
// + INVARIANTS P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable). The keyword IS the
// closed kind discriminator and lives on this enum.
//
//   N-input gates (§7.2):
//   - GateAnd, GateNand, GateOr, GateNor, GateXor, GateXnor
//   N-output gates (§7.2):
//   - GateBuf, GateNot
//   Three-state gates (§7.2):
//   - GateBufif0, GateBufif1, GateNotif0, GateNotif1
//   MOS switches (§7.5):
//   - GateNmos, GatePmos, GateCmos, GateRnmos, GateRpmos, GateRcmos
//   Bidirectional pass switches (§7.6):
//   - GateTran, GateRtran, GateTranif0, GateTranif1, GateRtranif0,
//     GateRtranif1
//   Pull gates (§7.7):
//   - GatePulldown, GatePullup
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 §7 admits this 26-way
// primitive-gate partition; SystemVerilog adds no new primitives.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every gate-emission consumer + every
//      per-gate terminal-arity check + every per-gate drive-strength
//      legality check reads the kind tag uniformly; shared
//      classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ keyword: String }` proxy admits
//      spec-illegal keywords (M4 closed-sets-are-enums-not-strings;
//      the K-1 readable-string heuristic surface this Disj forecloses).
//      A `{ id: Int }` proxy admits unbounded Int — same anti-pattern.
//      The enum IS the M4-faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the 26 gates are the
//      spec's primitive electrical-primitive taxonomy; the per-family
//      sub-partitions (n-input / n-output / three-state / MOS / pass /
//      pull) are observable derivations, not richer std/ algebra.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single gate instantiation has EXACTLY
//      ONE kind; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the 26 are structurally
//      heterogeneous per family (varying terminal-arity admissibility
//      per gate, varying drive-strength admissibility, varying
//      bidirectionality) — NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over
//      a separately-declared source set (Practice 7). The spec's
//      partition is irreducible.
// Terminal: the closed twenty-six-way Verilog primitive-gate-kind
// classifier of the IEEE 1364-2005 §7 spec.
type PrimitiveGateKind
  = GateAnd
  | GateNand
  | GateOr
  | GateNor
  | GateXor
  | GateXnor
  | GateBuf
  | GateNot
  | GateBufif0
  | GateBufif1
  | GateNotif0
  | GateNotif1
  | GateNmos
  | GatePmos
  | GateCmos
  | GateRnmos
  | GateRpmos
  | GateRcmos
  | GateTran
  | GateRtran
  | GateTranif0
  | GateTranif1
  | GateRtranif0
  | GateRtranif1
  | GatePulldown
  | GatePullup


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// GateInstantiation — a Verilog built-in primitive instantiation
// (`and g1(out, a, b);` / `not n1(o, i);` / `bufif1 b1(out, in, en);`,
// etc.). IEEE 1364-2005 §7. Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - gate_kind        which built-in primitive gate (PrimitiveGateKind
//                      closed Disj; replaces the prior free-String
//                      gate_keyword that admitted spec-illegal
//                      keywords — M4 / INVARIANTS P2 fix per codex
//                      BLOCKING on PR #3176)
//   - body_lexeme      instance name + terminal list + drive-strength
//                      + delay control as a verbatim lexeme (sub-
//                      grammar deferred — the per-primitive terminal
//                      arity / drive strength / delay control
//                      admissibility lives in the bundled T-4
//                      LanguageModel productions)
type GateInstantiation {
  gate_kind: PrimitiveGateKind
  body_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// UdpInstantiation — a Verilog user-defined-primitive instantiation
// (a UDP defined via `primitive ... endprimitive` at the file level
// per IEEE 1364-2005 §8 is instantiated like a gate at the module-
// body level). Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - udp_name         the UDP identifier (K-1-opaque Symbol;
//                      resolves through the namespace to the
//                      matching top-level UDP definition — UDP
//                      definitions themselves are a file-level
//                      concern, separate from module body items)
//   - instance_name    this instance's local name (K-1-opaque Symbol)
//   - body_lexeme      port list + drive strength + delay as a
//                      verbatim lexeme (sub-grammar deferred to the
//                      bundled T-4 LanguageModel)
type UdpInstantiation {
  udp_name: Symbol
  instance_name: Symbol
  body_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SpecparamDeclaration — a Verilog `specparam` declaration. IEEE
// 1364-2005 §14.3.1 + §12.2. Specify parameters are distinct from
// ordinary parameters: they are intended for timing-check values
// (rise/fall/hi-Z transitions, pulse-control limits) and CANNOT be
// used as bit-vector widths or array indices per spec. Conj record
// (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - name              the specparam identifier (K-1-opaque Symbol)
//   - default_lexeme    the value as a verbatim expression lexeme
type SpecparamDeclaration {
  name: Symbol
  default_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ModuleItem — the closed Verilog module-body item classifier. The
// items inside a `module ... endmodule` body are a flat list of
// concurrently-executing siblings (IEEE 1364-2005 §12.2); ModuleItem
// is the closed variant set of what each sibling can be.
//
// Per the IN-B PROBE FINDING above, this coproduct IS the parallel-
// composition surface: the module-body's `List<ModuleItem>` is the
// Conj children whose concurrent execution is the IN-B claim's
// structural representation — no 6th `Concurrent` behavior; the
// parallelism is the Conj-of-siblings shape, exactly as a Conj of
// submodule instantiations.
//
//   - ContinuousAssignItem        `assign net = expr;`
//   - AlwaysBlockItem             `always @(...) begin … end`
//   - InitialBlockItem            `initial begin … end`
//   - NetDeclarationItem          `wire`, `wand`, … declarations
//   - VariableDeclarationItem     `reg`, `integer`, … declarations
//   - ParameterDeclarationItem    `parameter NAME = expr;`
//   - LocalParameterDeclarationItem `localparam NAME = expr;` (§3.10)
//   - ModuleInstanceItem          nested `ModuleName inst (...);`
//   - DefparamDeclarationItem     `defparam top.sub.PARAM = value;` (§4.10.2.1)
//   - GenerateRegionItem          `generate ... endgenerate` (§12.4)
//   - SpecifyBlockItem            `specify ... endspecify` (§14)
//   - TaskDeclarationItem         `task name; … endtask` (§10.2)
//   - FunctionDeclarationItem     `function name; … endfunction` (§10.3)
//   - EventDeclarationItem        `event ev;` (§9.7.3)
//   - GenvarDeclarationItem       `genvar i;` (§12.4.2)
//   - GateInstantiationItem       `and g1(out, a, b);` (§7)
//   - UdpInstantiationItem        UDP instantiation (§8)
//   - SpecparamDeclarationItem    `specparam ...;` (§14.3.1)
//
// CLOSED-SET COVERAGE — the variants enumerated below cover the full
// IEEE 1364-2005 §12.2 module_or_generate_item + specparam_declaration
// grammar partition per Annex A.1.4. The prior 12-variant cut missed
// `event_declaration`, `genvar_declaration`, `local_parameter_
// declaration`, `gate_instantiation`, `udp_instantiation`, and
// `specparam_declaration` (codex BLOCKING on PR #3176, codex/codex-
// default 2026-05-16 — the strict spec-fidelity bar surfaced these
// gaps). Each new variant is a Conj record + body_lexeme deferral
// per the established AlwaysBlock body_lexeme precedent — the sub-
// language structures (gate-primitive terminal lists, UDP definition
// tables, generate-for elaboration, specify timing checks) are owned
// by the bundled T-4 LanguageModel productions when they land, but
// the variant + payload-skeleton lives here so the closed-Disj
// honestly covers §12.2 + §14.3.1. A NEW item kind (one the spec
// does not list) is the C1 stop-signal edit.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every module-body consumer (emission,
//      port-binding resolution, sensitivity-list signal lookup,
//      synthesis dispatch, generate-elaboration, specify-timing-
//      check, task/function inlining, gate-primitive emission, UDP
//      resolution, genvar elaboration) branches on the item kind;
//      shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: each variant carries a payload of
//      a DIFFERENT type (the 18 distinct payload records). Collapsing
//      to a uniform `{ kind: ItemKind, … }` shape requires Option-
//      typed payload fields, which makes mismatched-kind+payload
//      combinations representable — the inverse of P2.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the module-body items
//      are the IEEE 1364-2005 spec's primitive item taxonomy; they
//      do not trace to a richer std/ algebraic structure.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single item is EXACTLY ONE kind;
//      mutually-exclusive alternatives. (The module body as a
//      WHOLE is `List<ModuleItem>` — multiple items co-exist as
//      siblings, but each item is one kind. The Conj-of-siblings
//      IS the dimensional model the IN-B finding consumes; that
//      lives on `VerilogModule.items`, not on this enum.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the variants are structurally
//      heterogeneous (different payload types; the declaration
//      variants carry storage-shape facts while the action variants
//      carry computation-shape facts; gate / udp / specify / task /
//      function / generate each carry their own sub-language body)
//      — NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-
//      declared source set (Practice 7). The IEEE 1364-2005 module-
//      body item set is the irreducible partition.
// Terminal: the closed twenty-way module-body-item classifier of
// the IEEE 1364-2005 §12.2 + §14.3.1 spec (corrected per codex
// BLOCKING on PR #3176 2026-05-16 — added PortDeclarationItem
// (direction-only body port_declaration) and BodyPortDeclarationItem
// (body port_declaration with inline type, reusing the
// AnsiPortDeclaration carrier; the body-form combined declaration
// is structurally equivalent to ANSI per §12.3.4). The body port_
// declaration is itself a §12.2 module_item the spec lists; the
// prior 18-way cut missed both body forms.
// AnsiModuleItem — the closed Verilog module-body item classifier
// for ANSI-style modules (IEEE 1364-2005 §12.2 — the 18 items spec-
// admitted in any module body, EXCLUDING body port_declarations).
// ANSI ports are "completely declared" per §12.3.3, so body port_
// declarations (`PortDeclarationItem` / `BodyPortDeclarationItem`)
// are a SYNTAX ERROR in ANSI-style modules — they are admitted only
// in the NonAnsiModuleItem set below.
type AnsiModuleItem
  = ContinuousAssignItem { assign: ContinuousAssign }
  | AlwaysBlockItem { always_block: AlwaysBlock }
  | InitialBlockItem { initial_block: InitialBlock }
  | NetDeclarationItem { net: NetDeclaration }
  | VariableDeclarationItem { variable: VariableDeclaration }
  | ParameterDeclarationItem { parameter: ParameterDeclaration }
  | LocalParameterDeclarationItem { local_parameter: LocalParameterDeclaration }
  | ModuleInstanceItem { instance: ModuleInstance }
  | DefparamDeclarationItem { defparam: DefparamDeclaration }
  | GenerateRegionItem { generate_region: GenerateRegion }
  | SpecifyBlockItem { specify_block: SpecifyBlock }
  | TaskDeclarationItem { task: TaskDeclaration }
  | FunctionDeclarationItem { function_decl: FunctionDeclaration }
  | EventDeclarationItem { event_decl: EventDeclaration }
  | GenvarDeclarationItem { genvar_decl: GenvarDeclaration }
  | GateInstantiationItem { gate: GateInstantiation }
  | UdpInstantiationItem { udp_instance: UdpInstantiation }
  | SpecparamDeclarationItem { specparam: SpecparamDeclaration }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NonAnsiModuleItem — the closed Verilog module-body item classifier
// for non-ANSI-style modules. Non-ANSI bodies admit ALL AnsiModuleItem
// variants PLUS body port_declarations (PortDeclarationItem +
// BodyPortDeclarationItem) per IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.4 — port direction
// + (optional) type live in body items in the non-ANSI form. The
// Disj-with-named-payloads makes "ANSI port-list module with body
// PortDeclarationItem" structurally unrepresentable (codex BLOCKING
// fix on PR #3176 2026-05-16: items list was previously
// `List<ModuleItem>` regardless of port style, admitting the §12.3.3
// "ANSI ports are completely declared" violation).
type NonAnsiModuleItem
  = NonAnsiAnsiCompatItem { item: AnsiModuleItem }
  | NonAnsiPortDeclarationItem { port_decl: PortDeclaration }
  | NonAnsiBodyPortDeclarationItem { body_port: AnsiPortDeclaration }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ModuleParameterPortList — the closed Verilog module-header
// parameter-port-list classifier (IEEE 1364-2005 §12.1). A module
// declaration may carry an OPTIONAL `#(parameter W = 8, parameter
// D = 16)` clause between the module name and the port list. Per
// spec grammar the clause is module_parameter_port_list and admits
// parameter_declarations only (not localparam — localparams must
// live in the body). The optional-presence is structural and
// modeled via a closed Disj rather than a nullable field.
//
//   - NoModuleParameterPortList
//       — `module M (a, b);` — no `#(...)` clause; the module
//       takes no header-overridable parameters. (Body
//       ParameterDeclarationItem may still appear, but those
//       parameters are body-scoped and not part of the header-
//       overridable surface; a ModuleInstance's
//       ParameterOverrides bind to the HEADER parameter port list
//       per §12.1.3.)
//   - ModuleParameters { parameters: List<ParameterDeclaration> }
//       — `module M #(parameter W = 8, parameter D = 16) (a, b);`
//       — the header carries the parameter port list. These
//       parameters are externally overridable via instantiation
//       `#(...)` syntax (the ParameterOverrides Disj's actuals
//       bind positionally or by name to THESE header parameters,
//       not to body ParameterDeclarations).
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.1 admits exactly the
// present-or-absent partition; SystemVerilog adds type parameters
// and parameterized class declarations — a future language-version
// model would EDIT this Disj explicitly.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every module-instantiation /
//      parameter-resolution consumer reads the header parameter
//      port list to know which parameters are externally
//      overridable; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to a uniform `{
//      parameters: List<ParameterDeclaration> }` where empty-list
//      = "no clause" conflates "explicitly `#()` empty" (a syntax
//      no Verilog admits) with "no `#(...)` clause at all" — the
//      Disj makes the present-vs-absent partition structural.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the two variants are
//      the spec's primitive clause-presence partition; not a
//      richer std/ algebraic structure.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single module declaration has
//      EXACTLY ONE parameter-port-list-presence state; mutually-
//      exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: NoModuleParameterPortList is
//      nullary; ModuleParameters carries
//      `List<ParameterDeclaration>` — structurally heterogeneous,
//      NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over a single source
//      set (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed two-way Verilog module-header parameter-
// port-list classifier of the IEEE 1364-2005 spec §12.1.
type ModuleParameterPortList
  = NoModuleParameterPortList
  | ModuleParameters { parameters: List<ParameterDeclaration> }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// VerilogModule — a top-level Verilog module (`module Name
// #(parameters) (ports); items endmodule`). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.1.
// Per-style Disj — each variant carries its own header-port-list type
// AND its own admissible body-items list type. The §12.3.4 module-
// style partition is the dimensional axis of VerilogModule itself;
// "ANSI port-list module with body PortDeclarationItem" is
// unrepresentable by construction.
//
//   - NonAnsiVerilogModule
//       — non-ANSI style: header carries port-EXPRESSIONS only
//       (List<NonAnsiPort>); body carries the full NonAnsiModuleItem
//       set including body PortDeclarationItem +
//       BodyPortDeclarationItem per §12.3.4.
//   - AnsiVerilogModule
//       — ANSI style: header carries fully-declared ports
//       (List<AnsiPortDeclaration>); body carries AnsiModuleItem
//       only (the 18 items spec-admitted in any module body
//       EXCLUDING port_declarations — per §12.3.3 ANSI ports are
//       "completely declared" and body redeclarations are a syntax
//       error).
//
// Both variants carry name + parameter_port_list. The header
// parameter_port_list (`#(parameter W = 8)`) extends with body
// parameter declarations to form the module's UNIFIED OVERRIDEABLE
// parameter surface — instantiation `#(...)` overrides bind to the
// projection of header parameters concatenated with body
// ParameterDeclarationItems (in declaration order); body
// LocalParameterDeclarationItem is EXCLUDED from the overrideable
// surface per §3.10 (codex BLOCKING on PR #3176 — the prior framing
// suggested only header parameters were overrideable, but per spec
// both header and body `parameter` declarations are overrideable;
// only `localparam` is not).
//
// Per the IN-B PROBE FINDING: the `items` field IS the parallel-
// Conj surface; the structural concurrency of multiple always-blocks
// / continuous-assigns / instances is precisely "these are siblings
// in the items list". There is no `concurrent: Bool` flag; the
// parallelism is read FROM the list-of-siblings shape
// (feedback_no_annotations).
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.4 admits exactly the
// two module-style forms; SystemVerilog adds further declaration
// forms — future language-version model EDITS this Disj explicitly.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every module-level consumer
//      (emission, port-binding resolution, body item-set
//      admissibility check, synthesis dispatch) branches on the
//      style uniformly; shared classifier at the module-as-a-whole
//      level, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to a uniform Conj
//      record `{ name, parameter_port_list, ports: ModulePortsList,
//      items: List<UnifiedModuleItem> }` ratifies the §12.3.3
//      illegal state "ANSI ports + body port_declaration" — the
//      prior shape this VerilogModule Disj was introduced to
//      dissolve. The per-style payloads make admissibility
//      structural.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the two module styles
//      are the IEEE 1364-2005 spec's primitive module-style
//      partition; they do not trace to a richer std/ algebraic
//      structure.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single module declaration is EXACTLY
//      ONE style; mutually-exclusive alternatives per §12.3.4.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two variants carry
//      structurally heterogeneous payloads (NonAnsi.header_ports:
//      List<NonAnsiPort> + items: List<NonAnsiModuleItem> vs Ansi.
//      header_ports: List<AnsiPortDeclaration> + items:
//      List<AnsiModuleItem>) — the header port-list type AND the
//      body item-set type both differ per style. NOT one generic
//      `F<X>` / projection over a single source set (Practice 7).
//      The compound payload-type difference IS the partition's
//      defining structural fact.
// Terminal: the closed two-way Verilog module-declaration-style
// classifier of the IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.4 spec; ledger added per
// codex BLOCKING on PR #3176 2026-05-16.
type VerilogModule
  = NonAnsiVerilogModule {
      name: Symbol,
      parameter_port_list: ModuleParameterPortList,
      header_ports: List<NonAnsiPort>,
      items: List<NonAnsiModuleItem>
    }
  | AnsiVerilogModule {
      name: Symbol,
      parameter_port_list: ModuleParameterPortList,
      header_ports: List<AnsiPortDeclaration>,
      items: List<AnsiModuleItem>
    }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// VerilogCost — the per-target cost-realization carrier shape for the
// Verilog `LanguageModel`. Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
// This declares the SHAPE the U1 spine reads when computing Verilog-
// target realization cost — the scaffold header's "Cost realization:
// per-construct cost on a Verilog target" claim:
//
//   - gate_cost     per-construct gate-count cost (an XOR, AND, OR
//                   primitive's gate equivalent; the U1 per-target-
//                   op cost projection — the `map` side of
//                   `Homomorphism<…, …, VerilogBits>` carries this;
//                   cost is read FROM the map's structure, never
//                   stored as a parallel field per algebra.dag's U1
//                   no-engine discipline)
//   - area_cost     per-construct area cost (in technology-dependent
//                   units — gate equivalents in a generic process,
//                   LUT count on FPGA, transistor count on ASIC;
//                   the technology-specific cost table sits in the
//                   inhabitance walk that the T-4 LanguageModel
//                   shape parameterizes when it lands, not in this
//                   carrier itself)
//
// Both Int carriers are 🟡 scaffold (same bridge as VectorRange and
// ptx Dim3): cost is a non-negative quantity, but without
// std/cardinality.dag refinement the negative-cost state is
// structurally representable. Producer-side invariant pending the
// cardinality substrate landing.
type VerilogCost {
  gate_cost: Int
  area_cost: Int
}
